145:* **Machine shape:** Use `--high-mem` with `colab new`, `colab run`, or `colab ssh` (when auto-creating a runtime) to request a high-RAM machine shape. Requires Colab Pro or Pro+ entitlement for supported accelerators (CPU, T4, A100, etc.). L4 and TPU runtimes ignore this flag because they only offer one shape. Machine shape is shown in `colab sessions` and `colab status`.
146:* **TTY Requirements:** `colab repl` and interactive `colab ssh` expect a local TTY. When running inside automated scripts or pipelines, pipe stdin to `colab repl` (e.g., `echo "print(1)" | colab repl`) to trigger its non-interactive mode, or run one-shot shell commands with `colab ssh -s NAME -- CMD` (e.g., `colab ssh -s NAME -- ls /content`); pass untrusted values as separate arguments (after `--` where the program accepts it), since a single argument is a remote shell line. `colab ssh` needs a local `ssh` client and key, so where it can't work (no client or key, a tool not on the minimal ssh environment's `PATH`, or the runtime's concurrent SSH connections in use), pipe the commands to `colab exec` with IPython's `!` prefix (e.g., `echo '!ls /content' | colab exec`); each `!` line is its own shell and IPython expands `{expr}` and `$name` in it, so start a multi-line script with `%%bash` and keep untrusted text out of `!` lines.
147:* **Transparent Code Execution:** When calling `colab exec -f file.py`, the CLI reads the file locally and transmits its content to the remote kernel. You do not need to manually upload files before execution.
148:* **Storage & State Paths:** Session tokens and metadata are stored at `~/.config/colab-cli/sessions.json`. Global CLI settings are located at `~/.config/colab-cli/settings.json`. These can be customized or isolated via the global `--config` flag.
